tel 1.0

base locomotion
import soundness

info   Protocol Buffers from Scala types: numbered case-class fields as proto3 messages, with derived, direct and streaming codecs
detail
    _Locomotion_ encodes and decodes the Protocol Buffers wire format straight from Scala
    types. A case class whose fields carry proto3 field numbers *is* the message definition:
    there is no proto schema file to write and no code generator to run, and the bytes it
    produces are those `protoc` would produce for the equivalent schema. Field numbers come
    from the `field` annotation (or, unannotated, from declaration order); everything else —
    varints, zig-zag encoding, little-endian fixed values, length-delimited strings and
    nested messages, packed repeated fields, map entries and `oneof` variants — follows from
    the field types.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    case class Person(@field(1) name: Text, @field(2) age: Int)

    val bytes: Data = Person(t"Alice", 30).in[Protobuf].encode   // the wire bytes
    Chain(bytes).read[Person in Protobuf]                        // Person(t"Alice", 30)
    bytes.read[Protobuf].as[Person]                              // the same, in two steps
    ```

    The central type is `Protobuf`, a single wire value: `Wire(wireType, bytes)`, `Absent`,
    or `Repeated` of every occurrence of a field number. A whole message is one
    length-delimited `Wire`, and `Encodable in Protobuf` and `Decodable in Protobuf`
    instances — the scalar ones in `Protobuf`'s companion, the message ones derived through
    wisteria from the case class's shape — turn values into wire values and back. The sized
    numeric types of hypotenuse select the proto3 integer flavours by type alone: `U32` and
    `U64` are `uint` varints, `S32` and `S64` zig-zag `sint`s, `B32` and `B64` `fixed32` and
    `fixed64`. `Optional` is explicit presence, a `List`, `Set` or `Sequence` is a repeated
    field (packed when its element is `Packable`), a frozen `Array[Long]^{}` is the same
    field unboxed, and a `Map` is the standard repeated entry message.

    There are three paths through the library, which produce identical bytes:

    - **Derived**: `value.in[Protobuf]` builds the `Protobuf` tree through the derived
      `Encodable`, and `read[Protobuf].as[T]` walks the tree through the derived `Decodable`.
      Always available, and the reference the other two are tested against.
    - **Direct**: a `Protobuf.Parsable` reads a value's fields straight off the input through
      a `ProtobufReader` with no tree built, and a `Protobuf.Printable` measures and writes
      them straight into the output array. Both are generated at compile time by
      `Inlinable.parsable[T]` and `Inlinable.printable[T]`, by `derives Protobuf.Direct` on
      the message, or for every message and oneof in scope by `import codecs.direct`.
    - **Streaming**: `ProtobufParser` reads over a zephyrine `Cursor`, so a direct read over
      a `Chain[Data]` or a `Stream[Data]` reads each chunk as it arrives, holding only the
      field being read; a value split across chunks reads exactly as one that arrives whole.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import codecs.direct

    case class Point(@field(1) x: Int, @field(2) y: Int)

    val wire: Data = Point(3, 4).in[Data over Protobuf]   // printed directly
    wire.read[Point in Protobuf]                           // parsed directly
    ```

    `import codecs.derived` forces the derived path even where a direct codec is in scope,
    and `import codecs.strict` makes a `read[T in Protobuf]` or `in[Data over Protobuf]` on
    a type with no direct codec a compile error. The three modes are givens of distillate's
    `codecs` package, imported by name and one at a time.

    A malformed message raises `Protobuf.Error` (number 700), whose `Reason` names the fault
    and the byte offset — truncated input, a malformed or overflowing varint, an unexpected
    wire type, a missing `oneof` field. Field selection is by number, so the only optic is
    `fieldOptical`: `message.lens(_(Prim) = value)` replaces field 1 and passes every other
    field through, including ones the program does not know.

    The library has three components:

    - `locomotion.core` — `Protobuf` and its codecs, `field`, `WireType`, `Packable`, the
      streaming `ProtobufParser`, the `ProtobufReader` rim, `ProtobufPrinter` and
      `ProtobufSizes`, and `Inlinable` with its generators (the `inlining` object). Compiles
      for the JVM, Scala.js and Scala Native.
    - `locomotion.staged` — `InlinableStager`, the in-macro staging compiler which evaluates
      a *hand-written* `Inlinable` given at expansion time. It is found by name on the macro
      classpath; without it a field whose type has only a hand-written instance is decoded
      through its `Decodable in Protobuf` and encoded through its `Encodable`. Generated
      parsers and printers over case classes and sealed sums need only `core`.
    - `locomotion.http` — the `application/protobuf` media bindings: `postables`,
      `servables` and `construables` givens for telekinesis. JVM only.

    The umbrella exports `Protobuf`, `Protobuf2`, `Packable`, `WireType` and `field`, and
    mirrors the `conversions`, `postables`, `servables` and `construables` choice packages.
    `Inlinable`, `ProtobufParser`, `ProtobufReader`, `ProtobufPrinter` and `ProtobufSizes`
    are reached as `locomotion.Inlinable` and so on, since a generated codec names them for
    the user. Choice-package givens are imported by name —
    `import conversions.encodableToProtobuf`, `import postables.protobufPostable` — because
    `import pkg.*` does not import givens.

refer locomotion.Protobuf
refer locomotion.field
refer locomotion.Inlinable
refer locomotion.ProtobufParser
refer locomotion.ProtobufReader
refer locomotion.ProtobufPrinter

entry .Protobuf   a Protocol Buffers wire value, and the home of every codec into and out of the format
  detail
      `Protobuf` is an enum of the three shapes a field's recorded value can take, and the
      `Form` of `Encodable in Protobuf` and `Decodable in Protobuf`:

      - `Wire(wireType, bytes)` — one wire value: the payload bytes of a varint, a fixed
        32- or 64-bit value, or a length-delimited string, byte array, packed run or nested
        message, tagged with its `WireType`. A whole message is a `Wire(Len, bytes)`.
      - `Repeated(values)` — every occurrence of one field number in a message, in wire
        order, for repeated fields and for proto3's last-one-wins rule on scalars.
      - `Absent` — a field that was not present on decode, or should be omitted on encode.

      `#single`, `#occurrences`, `#payload`, `#wireKind` and `#isAbsent` read the three
      shapes uniformly, and `#as` decodes a value through any `Decodable in Protobuf`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Sample(@field(1) value: Int)

      val message: Protobuf = Sample(150).in[Protobuf]
      message.encode                     // Data: 08 96 01
      message.as[Sample]                 // Sample(150)
      ```

      The companion holds the codecs. The scalar instances are givens of the object itself
      (`intEncodable`, `textDecodable`, `u32Encodable`, …), as are the packed collection
      and frozen-array instances, `optionalEncodable`, `mapEncodable` and the mode
      selectors. The lower-priority layers `Protobuf2` (the derived codecs, the unpacked
      collection instances and the tree-materialising `read` path) and `Protobuf1` (the
      direct path, chosen whenever a `Protobuf.Parsable` or `Protobuf.Printable` is in scope)
      are traits the object extends, so their givens are members of `Protobuf` too and
      resolve from implicit scope with no import.

      A read is `bytes.read[Protobuf]` (`aggregable`, over any byte source) followed by
      `as[T]`, or the shorthand `read[T in Protobuf]`, which the layers route: through a
      `Parsable` when one is in scope (`Protobuf1#aggregableParsed`), and through the tree
      otherwise (`Protobuf2#aggregableIn`). A write is `value.in[Protobuf]`, whose `encode`
      is the message's bytes (`encodableInData`), or `value.in[Data over Protobuf]` for the
      bytes alone (`Protobuf2#encodableOver`). With a `Printable` in scope `in[Protobuf]`
      becomes `Protobuf1#encodablePrinted`: the printed array is the message's one `Wire`,
      so `encode` returns it without a copy.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label A message of one varint field: tag 0x08, then the varint 150
        code
            case class Sample(@field(1) value: Int)
            Sample(150).in[Protobuf].encode.to[List]   // List(0x08, 0x96, 0x01)
      frame
        label A string field is its tag, a length and the UTF-8 bytes
        code
            case class Person(@field(1) name: Text, @field(2) age: Int)
            Person(t"AB", 0).in[Protobuf].encode.to[List]   // List(0x0a, 0x02, 0x41, 0x42, 0x10, 0x00)
      frame
        label A nested message is length-delimited, and its field numbers are its own
        code
            case class Point(@field(1) x: Int, @field(2) y: Int)
            case class Wrapper(@field(1) point: Point, @field(2) label: Text)
            Wrapper(Point(3, 4), t"o").in[Protobuf].encode.to[List]   // List(0x0a, 0x04, 0x08, 0x03, 0x10, 0x04, 0x12, 0x01, 0x6f)
      frame
        label And back: the same bytes read as the type, through whichever path is in scope
        code
            Chain(Wrapper(Point(3, 4), t"o").in[Protobuf].encode).read[Wrapper in Protobuf]   // Wrapper(Point(3, 4), t"o")
      ##
      ```

      Every decoding given takes a `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`; the derived ones close over the
      tactic in scope at the derivation site, which is why a `summon` naming such an instance
      must allow its capture (`summon[(T is Decodable in Protobuf)^]`), while `read`, `as`
      and derivation need nothing special.
  refer locomotion.field
  refer locomotion.WireType
  refer locomotion.Inlinable
  refer locomotion.ProtobufReader
  refer locomotion.ProtobufPrinter
  entry #single   the most recent single wire value: proto3's last-one-wins rule for scalars
      A `Repeated` gives its last value (or `Absent` if it holds none); a `Wire` or `Absent`
      is itself.
  entry #occurrences   every wire value recorded for the field, for decoding `repeated` fields
      A `Repeated` gives its list, a lone `Wire` a one-element list, and `Absent` `Nil`. The
      unpacked collection decoders iterate this; a packed run arrives as a single `Len`
      occurrence which `ProtobufParser.packed` splits.
  entry #payload   the bytes of `#single`, or an empty array for an absent value
  entry #wireKind   the `WireType` of `#single`, or `Len` for an absent value
      Used to tell a packed scalar run (`Len`) from inline scalar occurrences when a
      repeated field of `Packable` elements is decoded, since a reader must accept both.
  entry #isAbsent   whether the value is `Absent` or a `Repeated` of nothing
  entry #as   decodes the wire value as a type, through its `Decodable in Protobuf`
      `message.as[Person]` is the second half of the two-step read
      `bytes.read[Protobuf].as[Person]`; it raises `Protobuf.Error` for a malformed payload.
      A derived decoder passes a field that occurs once as the `Wire` itself, one that
      occurs more than once as `Repeated` of every occurrence, and a missing one as
      `Absent`, so a hand-written `Decodable in Protobuf` should match through `single` or
      `occurrences` rather than on the `Repeated` constructor alone.
  entry .emit   a message's bytes as an iterator of blocks, produced on a fiber
      `Protobuf.emit(message)` hands the already-assembled payload out through a
      `Producer`, so that the bytes can be consumed as a stream; it needs a `Monitor` and a
      `Probate`. Protocol Buffers length-prefixes every nested message, so the bytes must be
      whole before any can be sent — this chunks them, it does not stream their production.
      For a value with a `Printable`, `Protobuf.Printable.emit` writes block by block instead.
  entry .aggregable   `bytes.read[Protobuf]`: any byte source aggregated as one message
      Reads the whole source into memory and wraps it as `Wire(Len, bytes)`; a `Protobuf`
      value is its bytes, so this holds the entire message, where a direct read through a
      `Parsable` holds only the field being read.
  entry .protobuf   `Protobuf is Decodable in Protobuf`: the identity, for a field typed `Protobuf`
      A field declared as `Protobuf` keeps its raw wire value, which is how unknown or
      pass-through content is carried through a message the program otherwise decodes.
  entry .encodableInData   `message.encode`: the wire bytes of a `Protobuf` message
      The `Encodable in Data` given, so the message's bytes are reached with the same verb
      jacinta uses for `Json`; `serialize` is reserved for the byte-to-text encodings.
  entry .abstractable   a `Protobuf` message as an HTTP body, with `application/protobuf`
      `Abstractable across HttpStreams to HttpStreams.Content`: the media type and the
      encoded bytes, from which telekinesis derives request and response bodies. The
      `locomotion.http` component's `postables`, `servables` and `construables` givens are the
      named bindings for a client post, a served response and a parsed request body.
  entry .chainReadable   `Chain(bytes).read[T in Protobuf]` reads a single-block chain in place
      The `Chain[Data] is Readable to (value in Protobuf)` given: a chain already in hand goes
      straight to the `Aggregable`'s own `aggregate`, which reads a one-block chain without a
      copy, rather than through a stream over the chain.
  entry .fieldOptical   `message.lens(_(Prim) = value)`: an `Ordinal` selects a field by number
      Protocol Buffers fields are numbered, not named, so an `Ordinal` is the optic: `Prim`
      is field 1, `Sec` field 2, and so on. Updating parses the message's fields, replaces
      the selected number's wire value with the transform's result (every occurrence, as a
      `Repeated`), and re-encodes the rest unchanged — including fields the program does not
      know. A number the message does not define, or a message that does not parse, leaves
      it as it was. The assigned value is a `Protobuf`; with
      `import conversions.encodableToProtobuf` any `Encodable in Protobuf` value may be
      assigned directly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import conversions.encodableToProtobuf

      case class Point(@field(1) x: Int, @field(2) y: Int)
      case class Wrapper(@field(1) point: Point, @field(2) label: Text)

      val wrapper: Protobuf = Wrapper(Point(3, 4), t"origin").in[Protobuf]
      wrapper.lens(_(Prim) = Point(7, 8)).as[Wrapper]   // Wrapper(Point(7, 8), t"origin")
      wrapper.lens(_(Sen) = Point(7, 8)).as[Wrapper]    // unchanged: there is no field 6
      ```
  entry .intEncodable   an `Int` as an `int32` varint
      Negative values take the full ten bytes, as proto3 specifies; a value expected to be
      negative is better typed `S32`, whose zig-zag encoding keeps it short.
  entry .longEncodable   a `Long` as an `int64` varint
  entry .booleanEncodable   a `Boolean` as the varint 1 or 0
  entry .doubleEncodable   a `Double` as a little-endian 64-bit value (`I64`)
  entry .floatEncodable   a `Float` as a little-endian 32-bit value (`I32`)
  entry .textEncodable   `Text` as its UTF-8 bytes, length-delimited
  entry .dataEncodable   `Data` as a length-delimited `bytes` field
  entry .sizedDataEncodable   a `Data & Sized[n]` block encodes exactly as the plain `Data`
      So a block whose size is proven at the type level encodes without losing the proof.
  entry .intDecodable   an `int32` varint as an `Int`; zero when absent
      Takes a `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`, raised for a truncated or malformed varint. Every
      scalar decoder reads an absent field as proto3's zero value.
  entry .longDecodable   an `int64` varint as a `Long`; zero when absent
  entry .booleanDecodable   a varint as a `Boolean`: any non-zero value is `true`
  entry .doubleDecodable   a 64-bit fixed value as a `Double`; `0.0` when absent
  entry .floatDecodable   a 32-bit fixed value as a `Float`; `0.0f` when absent
  entry .textDecodable   a length-delimited payload as `Text`, decoded as UTF-8
  entry .dataDecodable   a length-delimited payload as `Data`, the bytes as they are
  entry .u32Encodable   a hypotenuse `U32` as a `uint32` varint
      The sized integer types choose the proto3 integer flavour by type, with no annotation:
      `U32`/`U64` are plain `uint` varints, `S32`/`S64` zig-zag `sint` varints, and
      `B32`/`B64` little-endian `fixed32`/`fixed64`.
  entry .u64Encodable   a `U64` as a `uint64` varint
  entry .s32Encodable   an `S32` as a zig-zag `sint32` varint
      Zig-zag maps 0, -1, 1, -2, … to 0, 1, 2, 3, …, so a small negative stays a short varint
      where an `Int` would take ten bytes.
  entry .s64Encodable   an `S64` as a zig-zag `sint64` varint
  entry .b32Encodable   a `B32` as a `fixed32`, four bytes little-endian
  entry .b64Encodable   a `B64` as a `fixed64`, eight bytes little-endian
  entry .u32Decodable   a `uint32` varint as a `U32`; needs a `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`
  entry .u64Decodable   a `uint64` varint as a `U64`
  entry .s32Decodable   a zig-zag `sint32` varint as an `S32`
  entry .s64Decodable   a zig-zag `sint64` varint as an `S64`
  entry .b32Decodable   a `fixed32` as a `B32`
  entry .b64Decodable   a `fixed64` as a `B64`
  entry .optionalEncodable   an `Optional[T]` field: encoded when set, `Absent` when `Unset`
      Explicit presence: an unset field writes nothing, where a present-and-zero scalar
      writes its tag and value. Applies to any type `Mandatable` to its inner type, so
      `Optional[Text]`, `Optional[Point]` and `Optional[List[Int]]` all work.
  entry .optionalDecodable   an absent field reads as `Unset`; a present one through the inner decoder
  entry .packedEncodable   a collection of `Packable` scalars as one packed `Len` field
      proto3's default for repeated numbers: every element's encoding is concatenated into a
      single length-delimited field. Outranks the unpacked `Protobuf2#listEncodable`
      because `Packable` constrains the element; an empty collection is `Absent`.
  entry .packedDecodable   a repeated `Packable` field, from packed or unpacked occurrences
      Accepts both wire forms, as the proto3 specification requires of a reader: a `Len`
      occurrence is split with `ProtobufParser.packed` by the element's wire type, and any
      other occurrence is one element. Needs a `Factory` for the collection and a
      `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`.
  entry .packedListEncodable   the packed encoder for the prelude `List` of `Packable` elements
      The opaque prelude collections are not `Iterable`, so `List`, `Set` and `Sequence` each
      have their own packed pair, delegating to `packedEncodable` at the underlying type.
  entry .packedListDecodable   the packed decoder for the prelude `List`
  entry .packedSetEncodable   the packed encoder for the prelude `Set` of `Packable` elements
  entry .packedSetDecodable   the packed decoder for the prelude `Set`
  entry .packedSeriesEncodable   the packed encoder for the prelude `Sequence` of `Packable` elements
  entry .packedSeriesDecodable   the packed decoder for the prelude `Sequence`
  entry .intArrayEncodable   a frozen `Array[Int]^{}` as a packed field, with the `List` form's bytes
      A frozen array of packable scalars is the unboxed representation of a repeated
      numeric field: a generated parser fills it through an unboxed builder and a generated
      printer walks it by index, so no element is boxed on either path. The bytes are
      exactly those of the `List` form, so the two are interchangeable on the wire. One pair
      of instances per scalar, each pure like the scalar's own; `Array[Byte]^{}` is `Data`,
      a scalar.
  entry .longArrayEncodable   a frozen `Array[Long]^{}` as a packed field
  entry .booleanArrayEncodable   a frozen `Array[Boolean]^{}` as a packed field
  entry .doubleArrayEncodable   a frozen `Array[Double]^{}` as a packed field
  entry .floatArrayEncodable   a frozen `Array[Float]^{}` as a packed field
  entry .intArrayDecodable   a packed or unpacked repeated field as a frozen `Array[Int]^{}`
  entry .longArrayDecodable   a packed or unpacked repeated field as a frozen `Array[Long]^{}`
  entry .booleanArrayDecodable   a packed or unpacked repeated field as a frozen `Array[Boolean]^{}`
  entry .doubleArrayDecodable   a packed or unpacked repeated field as a frozen `Array[Double]^{}`
  entry .floatArrayDecodable   a packed or unpacked repeated field as a frozen `Array[Float]^{}`
  entry .mapEncodable   a `Map[K, V]` as repeated entry messages, `{ key = 1; value = 2 }`
      The proto3 map wire format: each entry is a length-delimited message whose field 1 is
      the key and field 2 the value, so a `Map[Text, Int]` is on the wire exactly what
      `map<string, int32>` would be. An empty map is `Absent`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Labels(@field(1) labels: Map[Text, Text])
      Labels(Map(t"a" -> t"b")).in[Protobuf].encode.to[List]   // List(0x0a, 0x06, 0x0a, 0x01, 0x61, 0x12, 0x01, 0x62)
      ```
  entry .mapDecodable   repeated entry messages as a `Map[K, V]`
      Each occurrence is scanned for its fields 1 and 2; an entry missing either decodes it
      as `Absent` — a scalar's zero, or the empty message. Needs a `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`.
  entry .aggregableDerived   under `import codecs.derived`, `read[T in Protobuf]` takes the tree path
      Outranks `Protobuf1#aggregableParsed` and applies exactly where it would — a type with a
      `Parsable` in scope — so the derived `Decodable` decodes even where a direct parser is
      in scope. Elsewhere the derived path resolves anyway.
  entry .readableDerived   under `import codecs.derived`, `data.read[T in Protobuf]` takes the tree path
  entry .encodableDerived   under `import codecs.derived`, `in[Protobuf]` derives even with a `Printable` in scope
  entry .aggregableStrict   under `import codecs.strict`, `read[T in Protobuf]` must find a `Parsable`
      The strict mode is the direct mode for code that must not fall back: this takes a
      direct parser wherever one resolves, and `Protobuf1#aggregableStrictFailure` reports a
      compile error naming the type wherever none does.
  entry .encodableOverStrict   under `import codecs.strict`, `in[Data over Protobuf]` must find a `Printable`
      `in[Protobuf]` itself is not gated: it names the wire value, as `in[Json]` names the
      tree.
  entry .Direct   `derives Protobuf.Direct`: a message's parser and printer generated once, into its companion
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass has no type parameter, so neither `Protobuf.Parsable` nor
        `Protobuf.Printable` can appear in a `derives` clause. `Protobuf.Direct[T]` is the
        carrier that can: `case class Foo(…) derives Protobuf.Direct` calls `.derived`, which
        generates `Foo`'s parser and printer with `Inlinable.parsable` and
        `Inlinable.printable` and stores them in the companion, where
        `Protobuf.Parsable.declared` and `Protobuf.Printable.declared` find them.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely

        case class Declared(@field(1) id: Int, @field(2) name: Text) derives Protobuf.Direct

        Declared(5, t"five").in[Data over Protobuf].read[Declared in Protobuf]
        ```

        The instances serve every read and write of the type with no import, and under
        `import codecs.direct` a type that derives `Protobuf.Direct` uses them rather than
        generating again at each use site, so the staging compile is paid once. Under
        `import codecs.derived` they are bypassed like any other direct codec.
    entry .derived   generates the type's `Parsable` and `Printable` and wraps them
    entry #parsable   the generated `Protobuf.Parsable`
    entry #printable   the generated `Protobuf.Printable`
  entry .Parsable   the direct-parsing typeclass: reads a value's fields straight off the input
    detail
        The counterpart of `Decodable in Protobuf` which never builds the tree: a `Parsable`
        receives a `ProtobufReader` whose window is set to the value's payload — the whole
        input at the top level, one field's wire value in a field position — and must
        consume to the window's end. It extends distillate's `Parsable` with `Transport =
        Protobuf` and `Reader = ProtobufReader`, so its one method is
        `#parse(reader: ProtobufReader^): Self`. The reader carries its own
        `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`, so a `parse` body needs no error vocabulary.

        A `Parsable` is the opt-in: with one in scope, `read[T in Protobuf]` resolves to
        `Protobuf1#aggregableParsed` or `Protobuf1#readableParsed` and reads directly; without
        one nothing changes. The ways to get one, in order of generality:

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import locomotion.Inlinable

        case class Person(@field(1) name: Text, @field(2) age: Int)

        given (Person is Protobuf.Parsable) = Inlinable.parsable[Person]   // generated, by hand
        val bytes = Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Protobuf].encode
        Chain(bytes).read[Person in Protobuf]                                // parsed without a tree
        ```

        or `derives Protobuf.Direct` on the message, or `import codecs.direct` for every
        message and oneof in scope (`Parsable2#generated`). A hand-written instance is
        `Protobuf.Parsable(reader => …)` (`.apply`), and `.fromDecodable` bridges any
        `Decodable in Protobuf` — the one-line escape hatch when a custom decoder must beat a
        generated parser.

        A generated parser reads a message's scalars in place, gathers a `List`, `Set`,
        `Sequence` or frozen array field occurrence by occurrence (packed or unpacked), reads
        a `Map` field entry by entry into the map's builder, dispatches a `oneof` by scanning
        the message for the lowest variant number present (its last occurrence), fills an
        absent scalar with its zero and an absent field with a declared default, skips an
        unknown field whole, and takes the last occurrence of a repeated scalar. A field
        whose type has no `Inlinable` — a sized integer, an `Optional` collection, a user
        type — is gathered as wire values and decoded once through its `Decodable in
        Protobuf`: the *seam*, which yields the same value more slowly.

        Over a `Chain[Data]` or a `Stream[Data]` the read never assembles the input first:
        the parser pulls each chunk as it needs it and holds at most the one field being
        read — except across a `oneof`, whose message is buffered while its variant is
        chosen. A read over a whole `Data` is read in place.
    refer locomotion.Inlinable
    refer locomotion.ProtobufReader
    refer locomotion.ProtobufParser
    entry #Transport   the transport type, `Protobuf`
    entry #Reader   the reader type, `ProtobufReader`
    entry .apply   a `Parsable` from a function over the reader
        `Protobuf.Parsable[Point](reader => …)`: the instance captures the function. Inside,
        the reader's `tag`, `enterField`, `varint`, `text` and `leaveField` walk the window;
        see `ProtobufReader`.
    entry .fromDecodable   a `Parsable` which materialises the window as a message and decodes it
        The bridge from the tree world: `reader.message()` copies the remaining window out as
        a `Wire(Len, bytes)` and the `Decodable` decodes it. This is what a type with only a
        `Decodable in Protobuf` reads through when a generated parser meets it as a whole
        value, and the way to make a custom decoder win over a generated parser.
    entry .declared   the parser a type generated into its companion with `derives Protobuf.Direct`
        Outranks `Parsable2#generated` (`import codecs.direct`), so a type that derives `Direct` uses
        the codec it generated once.
    entry .parseField   calls a nominal `Parsable` in a field position of a generated parser
        `Protobuf.Parsable.parseField[T](parsable, reader)` takes both as neutral `AnyRef`
        carriers — generated code is capture-erased — and reasserts them here, the audited
        point. Generated code calls it; hand-written code has no reason to.
    entry .Direct   the base class of generated parsers, taking the reader as a neutral carrier
      detail
          `Protobuf.Parsable.Direct[T]` implements `parse(reader: ProtobufReader^)` by
          passing the reader as an `AnyRef` to the protected `parseCarrier`, which generated
          code implements, reasserting the capability with `ProtobufReader.of` at each use. A
          generated override of `parse` itself would narrow the trait's `Reader^` parameter to
          a pure type, which capture checking rejects, so the carrier hop is the rim. Not
          needed by hand-written instances, which use `Protobuf.Parsable.apply`.
      entry #parse   `parseCarrier(reader)`, the reader passed as an `AnyRef`
  entry .Parsable2   the lower-priority layer holding `Parsable.generated`
    hidden
    detail
        The trait the object `Protobuf.Parsable` extends, so that the `import codecs.direct`
        given sits below `Parsable.declared`; `Protobuf.Parsable` inherits `generated` from it.
    entry #generated   under `import codecs.direct`, a generated parser for every message and oneof
        An inline given conditional on the `Codecs.Direct` mode and on
        `Inlinable.Generates[T]`, which holds only for a case-class message or a sealed sum of
        them; any other type — a generic case class, a collection — fails the search and keeps
        the derived path. The parser is expanded at each use site, which costs compile time;
        `derives Protobuf.Direct` pays it once.
  entry .Printable   the direct-printing typeclass: measures and writes a value's fields straight into the output
    detail
        The counterpart of `Encodable in Protobuf` which never builds the tree, in two
        passes: `#measure` walks the value once, recording every length-delimited size it
        meets in a `ProtobufSizes` table and returning the value's encoded length; `#write`
        walks it again, writing through a `ProtobufPrinter` and reading the sizes back in the
        same order. No field is encoded into an intermediate `Protobuf`, and no nested message
        is encoded and then copied into its parent. (protobuf-java memoizes a size on each
        message object; a case class has nowhere to keep one, hence the table.)

        With a `Printable` in scope, `value.in[Protobuf]` resolves to
        `Protobuf1#encodablePrinted`, whose message is the one `Wire(Len, bytes)` the printer
        filled, so `encode` returns the array uncopied; `in[Data over Protobuf]` gives the
        bytes alone, and `.print`, `.emit` run the passes explicitly.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import locomotion.Inlinable

        case class Person(@field(1) name: Text, @field(2) age: Int)
        given (Person is Protobuf.Printable) = Inlinable.printable[Person]

        Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Protobuf].encode              // written once, directly
        Protobuf.Printable.print(Person(t"Ada", 36))        // the same bytes
        ```

        The bytes are exactly those the derived `Encodable` writes — every scalar kind, text
        beyond ASCII, packed runs, maps, nested and repeated messages, a `oneof` in a field
        position, an optional message — so a message may be printed directly on one side of
        a connection and decoded through the tree on the other. The opt-in surface mirrors
        `Protobuf.Parsable`: an explicit instance, `Inlinable.printable[T]`, `derives
        Protobuf.Direct`, or `import codecs.direct` (`Printable2#generated`). A field whose type has no
        printing `Inlinable` is encoded once in the measuring pass through its `Encodable in
        Protobuf`, stashed in the table and copied out in the writing pass (`.fromEncodable`).
    refer locomotion.ProtobufPrinter
    refer locomotion.ProtobufSizes
    refer locomotion.Inlinable
    entry #measure   the value's encoded length, recording nested sizes in the table
    entry #write   writes the value through the printer, reading the sizes back
    entry .print   a value's wire bytes through its `Printable`: measured, then written once
        `Protobuf.Printable.print(value)` runs `measure` with a size table acquired from the
        thread (one per thread, reused, so a print allocates only its output), rewinds it,
        and writes into a `ProtobufPrinter.exact` array of the measured size, which is
        returned without a copy.
    entry .emit   a value's bytes in blocks: pushed to a consumer, or pulled as an iterator
        `emit(value, deliver)` measures as `.print` does and then writes through a
        `ProtobufPrinter.blocks` printer sized by the `Buffering` in scope, handing each
        block to `deliver` as it fills and the last by a `flush`; no array of the whole
        message is built, and a string longer than a block is delivered whole. `emit(value)`
        is the pull form: the blocks as an `Iterator[Data]^`, written on a fiber as they are
        consumed, needing a `Monitor`, a `Probate` and a `Buffering`; the `Printable` must be
        pure there, as a generated one is.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import strategies.throwUnsafely
        import locomotion.Inlinable

        case class Person(@field(1) name: Text, @field(2) age: Int)
        given (Person is Protobuf.Printable) = Inlinable.printable[Person]

        val blocks = scala.collection.mutable.ListBuffer[Data]()
        Protobuf.Printable.emit(Person(t"Ada", 36), blocks += _)
        ```
    entry .apply   a `Printable` from a measuring function and a writing function
        `Protobuf.Printable[T]((value, sizes) => …, (value, printer, sizes) => …)`; the
        instance captures both. The functions receive the `ProtobufSizes^` table and the
        `ProtobufPrinter^` as exclusive capabilities.
    entry .fromEncodable   a `Printable` which encodes the value once and writes its payload
        The bridge from the tree world: the measuring pass encodes the value through its
        `Encodable in Protobuf`, stashes the wire value in the table and returns the payload
        length; the writing pass unstashes it and writes the bytes raw. This is how a
        generated printer handles a field whose type does not print.
    entry .declared   the printer a type generated into its companion with `derives Protobuf.Direct`
    entry .measureField   calls a nominal `Printable`'s `measure` from a generated printer
        The carriers travel as `AnyRef` and are reasserted here, the audited point, as
        `Protobuf.Parsable.parseField` does for parsers. For generated code.
    entry .writeField   calls a nominal `Printable`'s `write` from a generated printer
    entry .Direct   the base class of generated printers, taking the printer and table as carriers
      detail
          `Protobuf.Printable.Direct[T]` implements `measure` and `write` by passing the
          `ProtobufSizes` and `ProtobufPrinter` as `AnyRef`s to the protected
          `measureCarrier` and `writeCarrier`, which generated code implements and which
          reassert the capabilities with `ProtobufSizes.of` and `ProtobufPrinter.of` at each
          use. Hand-written instances use `Protobuf.Printable.apply` instead.
      entry #measure   `measureCarrier(value, sizes)`, the table passed as an `AnyRef`
      entry #write   `writeCarrier(value, printer, sizes)`, both passed as `AnyRef`s
  entry .Printable2   the lower-priority layer holding `Printable.generated`
    hidden
    detail
        The trait the object `Protobuf.Printable` extends, so that the `import codecs.direct`
        given sits below `Printable.declared`; `Protobuf.Printable` inherits `generated` from it.
    entry #generated   under `import codecs.direct`, a generated printer for every message and oneof
        Conditional on `Codecs.Direct` and `Inlinable.Generates[T]`, exactly as
        `Protobuf.Parsable2#generated`.
  entry .EncodableDerivation   derives `Encodable in Protobuf` for a case class or a sealed sum
    detail
        The wisteria `Derivable` behind `Protobuf2#encodable`. A product encodes as a
        length-delimited message of its fields in declaration order, each numbered by its
        `field` annotation or, unannotated, by its 1-based position; an `Absent` field writes
        nothing and a `Repeated` one writes every occurrence. A sum encodes as a `oneof`: a
        message holding one field, numbered by the variant's 1-based position in the sum,
        whose payload is the variant's own encoding.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        enum Shape:
          case Circle(radius: Int)
          case Rectangle(width: Int, height: Int)

        (Shape.Rectangle(3, 4): Shape).in[Protobuf].encode.to[List]   // field 2: List(0x12, 0x04, 0x08, 0x03, 0x10, 0x04)
        ```
    entry .conjunction   the product encoder: numbered fields into one length-delimited message
    entry .disjunction   the sum encoder: the variant's message under its 1-based variant number
  entry .DecodableDerivation   derives `Decodable in Protobuf` for a case class or a sealed sum
    detail
        The wisteria `Derivable` behind `Protobuf2#decodable`. A product's payload is scanned
        once into a number-keyed table; each field's decoder receives its number's wire value
        — the `Wire` itself when it occurs once, `Repeated` of every occurrence otherwise, and
        `Absent` when missing, in which case a declared default is used first. A sum is
        decoded by the lowest variant number present in the message (its last occurrence);
        a message with no variant field raises `Protobuf.Error` with `MissingField`.
        Both close over the `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]` in scope at the derivation site.
    entry .conjunction   the product decoder: each field from its number, defaults for the missing
    entry .disjunction   the sum decoder: the lowest-numbered variant present, or `MissingField`
  entry .Error   raised when wire bytes are not a valid message
    detail
        Error 700: _the protobuf data was not valid: `reason`_. Raised through a
        `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]` by every decoding path — the derived decoders, the scalar
        decoders, `ProtobufParser`'s reads and a generated parser — and a `.Reason` says
        what was wrong and where. The offset on a byte-level reason is the position within
        the message or sub-message payload being parsed: on the streaming path it is the
        absolute offset in the input, on the tree path only the top-level message's offsets
        are absolute, since each nested payload is decoded from its own slice.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        case class Sample(@field(1) value: Int)

        // field 1, wire type Len, length 5, but only one payload byte present
        capture[Protobuf.Error]:
          Array[Byte](0x0a, 0x05, 0x41).read[Sample in Protobuf]
        // Protobuf.Error(Protobuf.Error.Reason.Truncated(2))
        ```
    entry #reason   why the data was rejected, and at what offset
    entry .Reason   the five faults a message can have
      detail
          Each case renders as its message:

          ```tabular
          column  Case
          column  Message
          column  Note
          row  `Truncated(offset)`  _the protobuf data ended unexpectedly at offset `offset`_  a length prefix, fixed value or varint ran past the end of its window
          row  `MalformedVarint(offset)`  _a varint was malformed at offset `offset`_  more than ten continuation bytes
          row  `UnexpectedWireType(found, offset)`  _an unexpected wire type, `found`, was encountered at offset `offset`_  a tag's wire code was 3, 4 (the deprecated groups), 6 or 7
          row  `Overflow(offset)`  _a numeric value was too large for its type at offset `offset`_  a tenth varint byte contributing more than bit 63
          row  `MissingField(number)`  _the required field number `number` was missing_  a `oneof` with no variant present; a decode-level fault, with no offset
          ```
      entry .communicable   renders each reason as the message above

entry .Protobuf2   the lowest codec layer: derived codecs, unpacked collections and the tree-reading path
  detail
      A trait the `Protobuf` object extends, so each of its givens is a member of `Protobuf`
      and resolves from implicit scope. It is the lowest of three layers — below `Protobuf1`
      (the direct path) and the object itself (scalars, packed collections and the mode
      selectors) — so that its blanket instances are chosen only when nothing more specific
      applies: the derived message codecs yield to a hand-written one, the unpacked
      collection instances to the packed ones for `Packable` elements, and the
      tree-materialising read to a direct parser. Exported to the umbrella because a
      derivation names it.
  entry #encodable   derives `Encodable in Protobuf` for any type with a wisteria `Reflection`
      An inline given which expands `Protobuf.EncodableDerivation` for a case class or a
      sealed sum; a type with neither a reflection nor another instance fails the search.
  entry #decodable   derives `Decodable in Protobuf` for any type with a wisteria `Reflection`
  entry #listEncodable   any `Iterable` as an unpacked repeated field, one tag per element
      The fallback for elements with no `Packable` instance — strings, byte arrays and
      nested messages — each written as a separate occurrence of the field. An element's
      own `Repeated` occurrences are flattened in, an `Absent` one is dropped, and an empty
      collection is `Absent`. Captures the by-name element codec.
  entry #listDecodable   any `Iterable` from its occurrences, through a `Factory`
      Decodes each occurrence with the element's decoder. A packed field still round-trips
      here when no `Packable` is in scope, since the element decoder reads one value.
  entry #aliasListEncodable   the unpacked encoder for the prelude `List`
      The opaque prelude collections do not conform to `Iterable`, so `List`, `Set` and
      `Sequence` each have an alias pair built at the underlying stdlib type.
  entry #aliasListDecodable   the unpacked decoder for the prelude `List`
  entry #aliasSetEncodable   the unpacked encoder for the prelude `Set`
  entry #aliasSetDecodable   the unpacked decoder for the prelude `Set`
  entry #aliasSeriesEncodable   the unpacked encoder for the prelude `Sequence`
  entry #aliasSeriesDecodable   the unpacked decoder for the prelude `Sequence`
  entry #arrayEncodable   a frozen `Array[T]^{}` of messages, text or bytes as an unpacked repeated field
      The bytes of the `List` form, so a repeated message field may be declared
      `Array[Message]^{}` and gathered by a generated parser with no cell per element. Needs
      a `ClassTag`. A frozen array of a `Packable` scalar packs instead, through
      `Protobuf.longArrayEncodable` and its siblings.
  entry #arrayDecodable   a repeated field as a frozen `Array[T]^{}`, for non-scalar elements
  entry #aggregableIn   `read[T in Protobuf]` through the tree: aggregate the bytes, then `as[T]`
      The `(value in Protobuf) is Aggregable by Data` given for any `Decodable in Protobuf`,
      capturing its `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`. A single in-memory block is decoded in place,
      a chain of several is gathered into one block first, and a stream is gathered as it
      arrives — in every case the whole message is held, since a `Protobuf` value is its
      bytes. `Protobuf1#aggregableParsed` outranks it whenever a `Parsable` is in scope.
  entry #encodableOver   `value.in[Data over Protobuf]`: the message's wire bytes
      The write-side mirror of `read[T in Protobuf]`: the payload of whatever `Encodable in
      Protobuf` resolves, so it prints directly where a `Printable` is in scope and encodes
      through the tree otherwise.

entry .Protobuf1   the middle codec layer: the direct path, taken whenever a `Parsable` or `Printable` is in scope
  detail
      A trait between `Protobuf2` and the `Protobuf` object. Its givens apply only to a
      type with a `Protobuf.Parsable` or `Protobuf.Printable` in scope, and then outrank
      `Protobuf2`'s tree-based ones, so a direct codec changes a read or write only where
      one exists. `import codecs.derived` overrides them from the object above, and
      `import codecs.strict` pairs the object's strict givens with the failures here.
  entry #encodablePrinted   `in[Protobuf]` through a `Printable`: the printed array as the message's one `Wire`
      The `Encodable in Protobuf` for any type with a `Protobuf.Printable` in scope,
      capturing it. The message is `Wire(Len, Printable.print(value))`, so `encode` returns
      the array without a copy. A lexically-scoped `Printable` — a local given, or one
      imported by name — makes the encoding direct.
  entry #aggregableParsed   `read[T in Protobuf]` over a chain or stream through a `Parsable`
      The `(value in Protobuf) is Aggregable by Data` given for any type with a
      `Protobuf.Parsable`, capturing the parsable and its `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`. A
      single-block chain is read in place; a longer chain or a stream is read through a
      `ProtobufParser` which pulls cells as it needs them, so the input is never assembled
      and at most one field is held — except across a `oneof`, whose message is buffered
      while its variant is chosen. Error offsets are absolute in the input.
  entry #readableParsed   `data.read[T in Protobuf]` over a whole `Data`, parsed in place
      Concrete in `Data`, so it beats the composed pipeline when the entire content is
      already in hand, and parses it without wrapping it in a one-element stream.
  entry #aggregableStrictFailure   under `import codecs.strict`, a read of a type with no `Parsable` fails to compile
      An inline given raising _`T` has no direct codec_ at the use site. It sits above the
      derived read so that the derived path cannot be taken silently, and below
      `Protobuf.aggregableStrict`, which takes every type with a parser first.
  entry #encodableOverStrictFailure   under `import codecs.strict`, `in[Data over Protobuf]` with no `Printable` fails to compile

entry .WireType   the four proto3 wire types: `Varint`, `I64`, `Len` and `I32`
  detail
      The low three bits of a field's tag say how its value is laid out: `Varint` (code 0)
      for `int32`, `int64`, `uint`, `sint`, `bool` and enums; `I64` (code 1) for `fixed64`,
      `sfixed64` and `double`; `Len` (code 2) for strings, bytes, nested messages, packed
      runs and map entries; and `I32` (code 5) for `fixed32`, `sfixed32` and `float`. The
      deprecated group types, codes 3 and 4, are not supported: a tag carrying either is
      rejected with `Protobuf.Error.Reason.UnexpectedWireType`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      WireType.Len.id            // 2
      WireType.fromId(5)         // WireType.I32
      WireType.fromId(3)         // Unset
      ```
  entry .fromId   the wire type with a 3-bit code, or `Unset` for 3, 4, 6 and 7
  entry #id   the 3-bit code packed into a field tag: 0, 1, 2 or 5

entry .Packable   marks a scalar whose repeated fields pack into one length-delimited run
  detail
      proto3 packs a repeated numeric field: every element's bytes are concatenated into a
      single `Len` field, rather than written one tag per element. `Packable` is the evidence
      that an element type packs, recording the `#wireType` its values use so a reader can
      split a packed run back into elements. Only numeric scalars have an instance — `Int`,
      `Long`, `Boolean`, `Float`, `Double` and the six sized integers — since strings, byte
      arrays and messages are length-delimited and always repeat as separate fields.

      The instances are givens of the companion, so a `List[Int]` field packs with no
      import, while a `List[Text]` field takes the unpacked `Protobuf2#listEncodable`. A
      reader accepts both forms whatever the writer chose.
  refer locomotion.Protobuf
  entry #wireType   how one element is laid out: `Varint`, `I32` or `I64`
  entry .int   `Int` packs as varints
  entry .long   `Long` packs as varints
  entry .boolean   `Boolean` packs as varints
  entry .u32   `U32` packs as varints
  entry .u64   `U64` packs as varints
  entry .s32   `S32` packs as zig-zag varints
  entry .s64   `S64` packs as zig-zag varints
  entry .float   `Float` packs as 32-bit fixed values
  entry .double   `Double` packs as 64-bit fixed values
  entry .b32   `B32` packs as 32-bit fixed values
  entry .b64   `B64` packs as 64-bit fixed values

entry .field   the `@field(n)` annotation: a case-class field's Protocol Buffers field number
  detail
      `case class Point(@field(1) x: Int, @field(2) y: Int)` declares `x` as field 1 and `y`
      as field 2, which is everything proto3 needs that the Scala type does not say. The
      numbers are read during derivation and by the generators, so the annotation is a
      `StaticAnnotation` with one `#number`. A field without one numbers itself by its
      1-based declaration position — what `protoc` would assign to the same declarations —
      and the numbers may be sparse, as protocol evolution requires:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Sparse(@field(3) a: Int, @field(7) b: Text)
      case class Unnumbered(first: Int, second: Int)   // fields 1 and 2

      Sparse(1, t"").in[Protobuf].encode.keep(2)   // the tag 0x18: field 3, varint
      ```

      Field numbers on a `oneof`'s variants are positional: the first case of the enum or
      sealed trait is field 1 of the `oneof` message, the second field 2, and the annotation
      is not consulted for them.
  entry #number   the field number, 1 or more

entry .conversions   givens that let an encodable value stand where a `Protobuf` is wanted
  detail
      A choice package, imported by name: `import conversions.encodableToProtobuf`.
  entry .encodableToProtobuf   a scoped `Conversion` from any `Encodable in Protobuf` value to its `Protobuf`
      Brings the implicit conversion `value => value.in[Protobuf]` into lexical scope, so an
      encodable value may be supplied directly at a lens assignment —
      `message.lens(_(Prim) = Point(7, 8))` — without writing `in[Protobuf]`. A conversion
      is never in scope by default; importing this is the explicit choice.

entry .Inlinable   the compile-time code generator behind direct parsers and printers, per field type
  detail
      `Inlinable` is the `Expr`-level counterpart of `Protobuf.Parsable` and
      `Protobuf.Printable`: a typeclass whose methods generate code at macro-expansion time.
      `#parse` receives an `Expr` of the reader, its window set to the value's payload, and
      returns an `Expr` of the decoded value; `#measure` and `#write` likewise return the two
      printing passes. The generator splices each result straight into the parser or printer
      it is building, so nested messages, repeated-field gathering, `oneof` dispatch and
      custom leaf codecs all inline into one flat method, with no runtime dispatch, no field
      maps and no adapter hops between composed parsers.

      Users meet it through two entry points:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import locomotion.Inlinable

      case class Person(@field(1) name: Text, @field(2) age: Int)

      given (Person is Protobuf.Parsable) = Inlinable.parsable[Person]
      given (Person is Protobuf.Printable) = Inlinable.printable[Person]

      Person(t"Ada", 36).in[Data over Protobuf].read[Person in Protobuf]
      ```

      Each generates a monomorphic instance for a case-class message or a sealed-sum
      `oneof`, composed from the `Inlinable` instances of its field types: the structural
      instances `derived` builds for nested messages and sums, the leaf givens of the
      companion (`int`, `long`, `boolean`, `double`, `float`, `text`, `data`) and `iterable`
      for repeated fields. A field type with no instance — a sized integer, an `Optional`
      collection, a user type — takes the *seam*: its occurrences are gathered as wire values
      and decoded through its `Decodable in Protobuf` (encoded through its `Encodable`), so
      every message still parses, only more slowly at that field.

      A hand-written instance (`given (MyLeaf is Inlinable) = new Inlinable { … }`) is an
      ordinary runtime value, and the generator can only call its `parse` by evaluating it
      inside the macro — which takes an in-macro staging compiler, contributed by
      `locomotion.staged` as a `.Stager`. Two constraints follow: the instance and its type
      must be compiled in an *earlier* run than the expansion (a same-run instance, with no
      trustworthy classfile yet, degrades to the seam), and without `locomotion.staged` on
      the macro classpath every hand-written instance degrades to the seam. Generated
      structural instances need neither. A message whose instance is a `derives` clause
      (`derives Inlinable.ForProtobuf`) is found in its companion by the staging summon.

      `Inlinable` is not exported to the umbrella: write `import locomotion.Inlinable`.
  refer locomotion.Protobuf
  refer locomotion.InlinableStager
  refer locomotion.inlining
  entry .parsable   `Inlinable.parsable[T]`: a generated `Protobuf.Parsable` for a message or oneof
      A macro. The type must be a case-class message, a sealed sum of them, or a type with
      an `Inlinable` instance; a collection is rejected (_a Protobuf message is the unit of
      direct parsing_), and a type with no instance and no message shape is a compile error
      suggesting a `Decodable in Protobuf`. The generated parser is bound to a lazy `self`,
      so a recursive occurrence of its own type — `Tree` with `children: List[Tree]` — reads
      through it in place.
  entry .printable   `Inlinable.printable[T]`: a generated `Protobuf.Printable` for a message or oneof
      A macro, as `.parsable`. A type whose instance does not print (`prints` is `false`) is
      a compile error: _`T` has no generated printer; a Protobuf message is the unit of
      direct printing_.
  entry .derived   the structural instance for a case-class message: `product is Inlinable`
      Reflects the product when its `parse`, `measure` or `write` is called — no macro of its
      own, since the `Type` arrives with the call. Fields are read by number, gathered,
      dispatched or seamed by their types.
  entry .ForProtobuf   the `derives Inlinable.ForProtobuf` carrier: a message's structural instance in its companion
    detail
        A `Self`-typed typeclass cannot appear in a `derives` clause, so `case class Foo(…)
        derives Inlinable.ForProtobuf` synthesises this parameterised subclass — which *is*
        a `Foo is Inlinable` — into `Foo`'s companion, where the expansion-time summon of
        another message's generator finds it. It delegates every method to the structural
        instance `Inlinable.derived` builds, and the resolution ladder unwraps it so the
        instance keeps its generator identity.
    entry .derived   wraps `Inlinable.derived[T]` for the `derives` clause
    entry #parse   delegates to the structural instance
    entry #absent   delegates to the structural instance
    entry #prints   delegates to the structural instance
    entry #measure   delegates to the structural instance
    entry #write   delegates to the structural instance
  entry .Generates   evidence that a type's codec can be generated structurally: a message or a oneof
    detail
        The condition on `import codecs.direct`'s generated instances. Its transparent given
        `.generates` is a macro which succeeds for a case-class message or a sealed sum of
        them, and fails — _`T` has no generated codec_ — for anything else, so the search for
        a generated instance fails and the derived path resolves instead. A cheap shape
        test, so a search under the mode stays cheap.
    entry .generates   the macro given: `Generates[T]` for a message or oneof, a failed search otherwise
  entry .Stager   the hook through which `locomotion.staged` evaluates a hand-written instance at expansion time
    detail
        `Inlinable.Stager` is the trait, with one method `#summon`, which `locomotion.staged`
        implements as `InlinableStager.Implementation`; the companion object loads that
        implementation by class name from the macro's own classloader when first asked, and
        `.summon` returns `None` when it is absent, so that core carries no dependency on
        the compiler jars. Looked up by name rather than through `ServiceLoader` so that the
        core compiles for Scala Native.
    entry .summon   the staged `Inlinable` for a field type, or `None` when `locomotion.staged` is absent
    entry #summon   evaluates the `Inlinable` given for a type inside a staging compiler
  entry .int   `Int` reads as a varint and prints as one; `0` when absent
      The leaf generators read the reader's window exactly as the tree accessors interpret a
      field's recorded payload, for hand-written instances that compose over one value.
  entry .long   `Long` reads and prints as a varint; `0L` when absent
  entry .boolean   `Boolean` reads and prints as a varint; `false` when absent
  entry .double   `Double` reads and prints as a 64-bit fixed value; `0.0` when absent
  entry .float   `Float` reads and prints as a 32-bit fixed value; `0.0f` when absent
  entry .text   `Text` reads the window as UTF-8 and prints through the size table's scratch
      The measuring pass encodes the string once into the `ProtobufSizes` scratch
      (`stashText`), recording its offset and length, and the writing pass copies it out
      (`ProtobufPrinter#stashedText`), so a string is encoded exactly once.
  entry .data   `Data` reads the window as bytes and prints them raw
  entry .iterable   carries the element's instance for a repeated field; never parses alone
      A collection's occurrences may be scattered through the whole message, and packed, so
      a collection cannot be parsed from a single field window: gathering lives in the
      message generator. This instance only carries the element's `Inlinable` to it, and
      its `parse` is a compile error if reached directly.
  entry #parse   generates the code which reads `Self` from the reader's window
  entry #prints   whether the instance generates a printer; `false` by default
      An instance that does not print — the default, so a parse-only instance stays honest
      — puts its field on the `Encodable in Protobuf` seam in a generated printer.
  entry #measure   generates the measuring pass: records nested sizes, returns the encoded length
      The default is a compile error, _the Inlinable for `T` generates no printer_; an
      instance overriding `prints` must override this and `#write`. The table travels as an
      `Expr[AnyRef]`, reasserted with `ProtobufSizes.of`.
  entry #write   generates the writing pass through the printer, reading sizes back
      The printer and table travel as `Expr[AnyRef]`s, reasserted with `ProtobufPrinter.of`
      and `ProtobufSizes.of`, since generated code is capture-erased.
  entry #absent   generates what a field of this type yields when its number is missing
      proto3 has no required fields: the leaf instances yield their zero, a message instance
      an all-fields-absent record, and the default aborts with
      `Protobuf.Error.Reason.MissingField(0)` through the given tactic, so a custom instance
      which does not override it is loud rather than silently wrong.

entry .ProtobufParser   the streaming wire parser: reads fields straight off a `Cursor`, one window at a time
  detail
      `ProtobufParser` reads Protocol Buffers wire bytes off a zephyrine `Cursor`, one field
      at a time, so a message arriving in chunks is read as it arrives and the buffer holds
      only the field being read. It is a stateful, exclusive capability (`ProtobufParser^`),
      constructed only through the companion's `.apply` over a whole `Data` (read in place,
      with no cursor), a `Chain[Data]` (cells pulled as needed) or a `Stream[Data]`
      (chunks read as they are delivered), and owned by one read for its duration.

      Reads are bounded by a *window*: the extent of the value being parsed, as an absolute
      offset in the input, so nested messages parse in place rather than over a copied
      payload. `#directEnterField` narrows the window to one field's wire value by its tag's
      wire code and `#directLeaveField` restores the enclosing one, skipping whatever of the
      field remains; every scalar read checks the window and reports a `Protobuf.Error`
      with the absolute offset. A `oneof` is read by buffering the window whole
      (`#directBufferWindow`), scanning it for the variant, and re-entering the variant's
      extent (`#directWindow`, `#directRestore`).

      Generated parsers, spliced into user modules, bind the parser once per record and read
      through this *direct rim* — the `direct…` methods, each taking a
      `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]` — which is why they are public. Hand-written `Parsable`
      instances use `ProtobufReader`, the restricted rim which carries the tactic for them.
      The companion also holds the whole-payload reads the tree path uses: `.fields`,
      `.packed`, `.varintOf`, `.fixed32Of` and `.fixed64Of`, each over an in-memory `Data`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      // Field 1 (tag 0x0a), length 3, "ABC"
      val parser = ProtobufParser(Array[Byte](0x0a, 0x03, 0x41, 0x42, 0x43))
      val tag = parser.directTag()                       // 0x0a: field 1, wire code 2
      val saved = parser.directEnterField(tag & 7)
      val text = parser.directStringWindow()             // "ABC"
      parser.directLeaveField(saved)
      parser.directAtLimit                               // true
      ```

      Performance notes a user may rely on: a one-byte varint (a tag, a bool, a short length)
      is read in place; two- to four-byte varints take a straight-line path; a string field
      whose bytes are all ASCII, read over a whole `Data`, is decoded through a Latin-1 copy
      rather than the UTF-8 constructor, with an identical `Text`; and a read over a whole
      `Data` has no cursor at all. Window positions cross the rim as `Int`s: a message is at
      most 2 GiB, as its `int32` length prefixes require.
  refer locomotion.ProtobufReader
  refer locomotion.Protobuf
  entry .apply   a parser over a whole `Data`, a `Chain[Data]`, or a consumed `Stream[Data] over Credit`
      Over a `Data` the array is the buffer, borrowed and never written, and the window is
      its length. Over a chain or a stream the window is unbounded — the message ends where
      the input ends, which a stream reveals only by ending — and cells are pulled as the
      parser needs them. The stream overload takes its argument as `consume`: the stream is
      used exactly once, by the parser.
  entry .of   reasserts a parser that travelled as a neutral `AnyRef` carrier, as `ProtobufParser^`
      For generated parsers, which receive the reader's parser as a carrier and need the
      exclusive capability for every `update` call; inline, so the cast expression itself
      lands in the user module. Hand-written code has no carrier to reassert.
  entry .varintOf   a varint from the start of an in-memory payload; needs a `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]`
      The allocation-free scalar read of the tree path: `Protobuf.intDecodable` and its
      siblings call it on a field's recorded payload. A one-byte varint is read in place;
      more than ten bytes is `MalformedVarint`, running out is `Truncated`, and a tenth byte
      contributing more than bit 63 is `Overflow`.
  entry .fixed32Of   a little-endian 32-bit value from a payload; `Truncated` if shorter than four bytes
  entry .fixed64Of   a little-endian 64-bit value from a payload; `Truncated` if shorter than eight bytes
  entry .fields   a message payload as a map from field number to every occurrence, in first-occurrence order
      Walks the payload once and copies each field's payload into its own `Protobuf.Wire`,
      preserving repeats and unknown fields. `fieldOptical` uses it to rewrite one field and
      pass the rest through. A field's occurrences are listed in wire order.
  entry .packed   splits a packed run into one wire value per element, by the element's wire type
      `ProtobufParser.packed(payload, WireType.Varint)` splits a `Len` payload of
      concatenated varints; `I32` and `I64` split at fixed widths. The wire type comes from
      the element's `Packable` instance. A truncated final element is `Truncated`.
  entry #directAtLimit   whether the window has no more content; at the top level, whether the input has ended
      The record loop's test: `while !parser.directAtLimit` reads tags until the window is
      consumed. On an unbounded window it asks the cursor for more before answering, so a
      live stream's complete field reads without waiting for the next.
  entry #directMark   the current absolute offset, as an `Int`
  entry #directBoundary   the window's end offset, as an `Int`; `Int.MaxValue` when unbounded
  entry #directTag   the next field's tag, `(number << 3) | wireCode`; call only below the limit
  entry #directVarint   a window-bounded varint
      `Truncated` at the window's end or the input's, `MalformedVarint` past ten bytes,
      `Overflow` for a tenth byte above bit 63; the offset in each is where the varint began.
  entry #directFixed32   four little-endian bytes as an `Int`, within the window
  entry #directFixed64   eight little-endian bytes as a `Long`, within the window
  entry #directEnterField   narrows the window to one field's value by its wire code; returns the enclosing boundary
      Code 0 covers the varint's own bytes, 1 the next eight, 5 the next four, and 2
      consumes the length prefix and covers that many bytes; code 3, 4, 6 or 7 is
      `UnexpectedWireType` at the tag's offset. A field which would run past the enclosing
      window is `Truncated`; an unbounded enclosing window cannot prove the bytes exist on
      entry, so its reads report the truncation instead, at the same offset. The returned
      token is for `#directLeaveField`.
  entry #directLeaveField   restores the enclosing window, skipping whatever of the field remains
      A parse may legitimately read less than the field's payload — the tree accessors
      ignore a payload's trailing bytes too — so the remainder is skipped, not buffered.
  entry #directSkipField   skips one field whole by its wire code: enter, then leave
      How a generated parser passes over a field number it does not know.
  entry #directLong   a field's value as a `Long`, by its wire code
      With code 0 the varint is read in place; with any other code the field's window is
      entered and its payload interpreted as the tree accessor would interpret the recorded
      payload — a `Len` payload of one varint reads as that varint.
  entry #directDouble   a field's value as a `Double`: in place for code 1, through the window otherwise
  entry #directFloat   a field's value as a `Float`: in place for code 5, through the window otherwise
  entry #directString   a length-delimited field's content as a `String`, fused for code 2
      For code 2 the length prefix, bounds check and UTF-8 decode are one step; any other
      code takes the window path.
  entry #directData   a length-delimited field's content copied out as `Data`
  entry #directWire   one field's value materialised as a `Protobuf.Wire`
      The runtime seam for a field type without an `Inlinable`: each occurrence is gathered
      this way and the field decoded once through its `Decodable in Protobuf`, exactly as
      the tree path decodes `Repeated(values)`.
  entry #directStringWindow   the whole current window as a `String`
      The window-level read behind `ProtobufReader#text`, for a `parse` body that owns the
      field's window already.
  entry #directDataWindow   the whole current window copied out as `Data`
  entry #directMessage   the remaining window as a length-delimited `Protobuf.Wire` message
      The whole-value seam behind `Protobuf.Parsable.fromDecodable`.
  entry #directBufferWindow   buffers the window's remaining content whole, before a oneof scan
      An unbounded window becomes bounded by the input's end, which the fill has found;
      afterwards neither a scan nor a re-entry can refill — and so compact — the buffer, so
      offsets returned by `#directMark` stay valid for `#directWindow` until the window is
      left. Must precede `#directWindow` and `#directRestore`.
  entry #directWindow   moves to an absolute extent within the buffered window; returns a token for `#directRestore`
      `directWindow(start, end)` seeks to `start` and bounds the window at `end` — a
      variant's recorded extent — packing the previous position and boundary into the
      returned `Long`. Only after `#directBufferWindow`.
  entry #directEmptyWindow   an empty window at the read position, from which an absent nested value parses
      An absent message field parses as the empty message, so a generated parser enters an
      empty window and runs the nested parser over it. Restored by `#directRestore`.
  entry #directRestore   restores the position and boundary a `#directWindow` or `#directEmptyWindow` token holds

entry .ProtobufReader   the restricted rim a `Protobuf.Parsable` reads through: a window, its fields and their scalars
  detail
      `ProtobufReader` is what a `Protobuf.Parsable.parse` receives: the parser with its
      window set to the value's payload, and a `Tactic[Protobuf.Error]` carried inside, so a
      `parse` body needs no error vocabulary — malformed input aborts through the read's
      ambient tactic. Only locomotion's read paths construct one; it is an exclusive,
      stateful capability owned by one `parse` call for its duration, and nothing of it may
      be retained afterwards.

      A hand-written instance walks its window with `#more`, `#tag`, `#enterField` and
      `#leaveField`, reading each field's scalar in between:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      case class Point(@field(1) x: Int, @field(2) y: Int)

      given (Point is Protobuf.Parsable) = Protobuf.Parsable: reader =>
        var x = 0
        var y = 0

        while reader.more do
          val tag = reader.tag()
          val saved = reader.enterField(tag & 7)
          (tag >>> 3) match
            case 1 => x = reader.varint().toInt
            case 2 => y = reader.varint().toInt
            case _ => ()
          reader.leaveField(saved)

        Point(x, y)

      Point(3, 4).in[Protobuf].encode.read[Point in Protobuf]   // Point(3, 4)
      ```

      The scalar readers (`#varint`, `#fixed32`, `#fixed64`, `#text`, `#data`) read the
      window's content exactly as the tree accessors interpret a field's recorded payload,
      so an instance composed over one wire value agrees with the derived decoder. `#wire`
      and `#message` are the seams back to the tree world. Generated parsers do not use these
      forwarders: they take the parser itself through `ProtobufReader.of` and read through
      `ProtobufParser`'s direct rim.
  refer locomotion.ProtobufParser
  refer locomotion.Protobuf
  entry .of   reasserts a reader that travelled as a neutral `AnyRef` carrier, as `ProtobufReader^`
      The entry of a generated parser's `parseCarrier(reader: AnyRef)`, spliced into the
      user's module; inline so the cast lands there, where a method's `^` result would be
      read-only in a module that is capture-checked but not separation-checked.
  entry #more   whether the window has more content: the record loop's test
  entry #tag   the next field's tag, `(number << 3) | wireCode`
  entry #enterField   narrows the window to the next field's value by its wire code; returns the token to leave with
  entry #leaveField   restores the enclosing window, skipping the rest of the field
  entry #skipField   skips the next field whole, by its wire code
  entry #varint   a varint from the window
  entry #fixed32   four little-endian bytes as an `Int`
  entry #fixed64   eight little-endian bytes as a `Long`
  entry #text   the whole window as UTF-8 `Text`
  entry #data   the whole window copied out as `Data`
  entry #wire   one field's value materialised as a `Protobuf`, for decoding through a `Decodable in Protobuf`
  entry #message   the remaining window as a `Protobuf` message, for `Protobuf.Parsable.fromDecodable`

entry .ProtobufPrinter   writes wire bytes into a buffer it owns: exact-size, growable or block by block
  detail
      `ProtobufPrinter` writes Protocol Buffers wire bytes: varints as LEB128, fixed values
      little-endian, a field as its tag followed by its value (length-prefixed for `Len`).
      It owns its buffer in one of three modes, chosen by the companion's constructor:
      `.exact(size)` is an array of exactly the measured size, which `#data` returns without
      a copy; `.growing(hint)` doubles as needed, for bytes of unknown length; and
      `.blocks(size, deliver)` hands each filled block to `deliver` as the next write would
      overflow it, with `#flush` handing on the last partial one, so a message is written
      as a stream of blocks and never as one array. A write longer than a whole block goes
      to `deliver` directly. The printer is a stateful, exclusive capability
      (`ProtobufPrinter^`) with update-classified writes.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val printer = ProtobufPrinter.exact(3)
      printer.tag(1, WireType.Varint)
      printer.varint(150L)
      printer.data().to[List]   // List(0x08, 0x96, 0x01)
      ```

      The companion's size functions — `.varintSize`, `.tagSize`, `.utf8Length` and
      `.fieldSize` — are what a `Printable`'s measuring pass sums, and `#field` mirrors
      `.fieldSize` byte for byte: writing past an exact-size printer's end throws
      `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`, so a measure that disagrees with its write is loud.
      `.varintData` and `.fixedData` are the scalar encoders of the tree path.
  refer locomotion.ProtobufSizes
  refer locomotion.Protobuf
  entry .exact   a printer of exactly `size` bytes, whose `#data` is that array, uncopied
  entry .growing   a printer over a growable buffer, starting at `hint` bytes (at least 16)
  entry .blocks   a printer that hands each filled block of `size` bytes to a consumer
      `deliver` is taken as `consume`: it is owned by the printer. Blocks are at least 16
      bytes. `#flush` delivers the final partial block; `Protobuf.Printable.emit` is the
      usual caller.
  entry .of   reasserts a printer that travelled as a neutral `AnyRef` carrier, as `ProtobufPrinter^`
      For generated printers, whose `writeCarrier` receives the printer as an `AnyRef`;
      inline, as `ProtobufParser.of` is.
  entry .varintSize   the LEB128 length of a value read as unsigned 64-bit: 1 to 10 bytes
  entry .utf8Length   the length of a string's UTF-8 encoding, without encoding it
  entry .tagSize   the length of a field's tag: one byte up to field 15, a varint thereafter
  entry .fieldSize   the number of bytes `#field` writes for a number and a wire value
      `Absent` is 0, `Repeated` the sum of its values, and a `Wire` its tag plus — for `Len` —
      a length prefix, plus the payload.
  entry .varintData   a value's LEB128 bytes as a `Data` of exactly their length
  entry .fixedData   the low `width` bytes of a value, little-endian, as a `Data`
  entry #data   the bytes written, in exact-size or growable mode
      An exact-size printer whose size has been reached returns its array without a copy;
      otherwise the bytes written so far are copied out.
  entry #flush   in block mode, hands the bytes written so far to the consumer as a block
  entry #byte   writes one byte
  entry #raw   writes bytes as they are
      The common case — the bytes fit — is one comparison and a copy; in block mode a
      payload longer than a block is flushed around and delivered whole.
  entry #chunk   writes a length-delimited payload: its length as a varint, then its bytes
  entry #varint   writes a value as a LEB128 varint, 1 to 10 bytes
  entry #utf8   writes a string's UTF-8 encoding straight into the output
      Its length, which a printer measures first, is `ProtobufPrinter.utf8Length`.
  entry #stashedText   writes a string the measuring pass stashed: its length prefix, then its bytes from the table
      Reads the next offset and length from the `ProtobufSizes` and copies the bytes out of
      the table's scratch; the counterpart of `ProtobufSizes#stashText`.
  entry #fixed32   writes an `Int` as four little-endian bytes
  entry #fixed64   writes a `Long` as eight little-endian bytes
  entry #tag   writes a field tag, `(number << 3) | wireType.id`, as a varint
  entry #field   writes a numbered field from a `Protobuf` value: tag, length prefix for `Len`, payload
      `Absent` writes nothing, `Repeated` writes every value under the same number, and a
      `Wire` writes its tag and bytes. Exactly `ProtobufPrinter.fieldSize` bytes.

entry .ProtobufSizes   the size table between a direct printer's measuring and writing passes
  detail
      Every length-delimited value — a nested message, a string, a packed run — needs its
      size as a prefix before its bytes, so a `Protobuf.Printable` measures a value in one
      pass, recording each such size here in the order it is visited, and writes it in a
      second, reading the sizes back in the same order (`#rewind`, then `#next`). A nested
      message's slot is reserved before its children are measured (`#reserve`) and filled
      after (`#set`), so the table is in pre-order on both passes. What the measuring pass
      produces and the writing pass needs is kept alongside: a string is encoded once into
      the table's byte scratch (`#stashText`) and copied out by `ProtobufPrinter#stashedText`,
      and a wire value a seam encoded is stashed as a reference (`#stash`, `#unstash`).

      A stateful, exclusive capability (`ProtobufSizes^`), owned by one print for its
      duration: `Protobuf.Printable.print` takes the thread's table, reused by every print on
      that thread so a print allocates nothing but its output, and takes a fresh one if the
      thread's is busy — a seam's `Encodable` printing a nested message through its own
      `Printable`. Hand-written `Printable` instances receive it as a parameter and need only
      pass it on to the field codecs they compose.
  refer locomotion.ProtobufPrinter
  refer locomotion.Protobuf
  entry .of   reasserts a table that travelled as a neutral `AnyRef` carrier, as `ProtobufSizes^`
      For generated printers; inline, as `ProtobufParser.of` is.
  entry #reserve   a slot for a size known only after its children are measured; returns its index
  entry #set   fills a reserved slot
  entry #record   records a size in the next slot: `set(reserve(), size)`
  entry #rewind   moves the read position to the start, for the writing pass
  entry #next   the next recorded size, in the order it was recorded
  entry #stashText   encodes a string into the scratch, recording its offset and length; returns the encoded length
      The measuring half of writing a string once; `ProtobufPrinter#stashedText` is the
      writing half, reading the two recorded values back through `#next`.
  entry #stash   keeps a reference for the writing pass, in order
      `Protobuf.Printable.fromEncodable` stashes the `Protobuf` it encoded while measuring.
  entry #unstash   the next stashed reference, in the order stashed

entry .inlining   the macro implementations behind `Inlinable.parsable`, `Inlinable.printable` and `codecs.direct`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object holds the expansion-time instance resolution, the
      structural generators for messages, repeated and map fields and `oneof` sums, and the
      three entry macros. The generated parser or printer is bound to a lazy `self` so a
      recursive type reads through it in place, and a type refined with a `Form` member — a
      `Foo in Protobuf` read infers `Foo { type Form = Protobuf }` — generates `Foo`'s codec.
  entry .inlinableParsable   the macro behind `Inlinable.parsable[T]`
  entry .inlinablePrintable   the macro behind `Inlinable.printable[T]`
  entry .generates   the macro behind `Inlinable.Generates.generates`: succeeds for a message or oneof

entry .InlinableStager   the in-macro staging compiler which evaluates a hand-written `Inlinable` at expansion time
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The `locomotion.staged` component's one object: `Inlinable.Stager`
      loads `.Implementation` by name when `locomotion.staged` is on the macro classpath, and
      `.evaluate` runs a fresh staging compiler — over the macro classloader's classpath with
      the current output directory excluded — to summon the `Inlinable` given for a field
      type and hand the live instance back to the generator. Only a class-shaped type (a
      class or an application of one) whose classfile already exists can cross into the
      inner compiler: an opaque or structural type, or one defined in the compilation run
      in progress, yields `None`, and the field takes the runtime seam through its
      `Decodable`/`Encodable in Protobuf`. Each summon reports its duration as a compiler
      info message, _locomotion: staged summon for `T` took `n`ms_.
  entry .evaluate   the staged summon: `Some(instance)` for an evaluable type, `None` otherwise
  entry .Implementation   the `Inlinable.Stager` that `Inlinable.Stager.summon` delegates to
    entry .summon   calls `InlinableStager.evaluate` for the type

entry .postables   the given which posts a `Protobuf` message as an HTTP request body
  detail
      A choice package of `locomotion.http` (JVM only), merged with the umbrella's own, so
      the given is imported by name: `import postables.protobufPostable`.
  entry .protobufPostable   `Protobuf is Postable`: the wire bytes with the media type `application/protobuf`
      Protocol Buffers is binary, so the body is the message's bytes and no charset is
      named. With it in scope a `Protobuf` value may be the body of a telekinesis `post`
      or `put`:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import postables.protobufPostable

      case class Person(@field(1) name: Text, @field(2) age: Int)
      val body: Protobuf = Person(t"Alice", 30).in[Protobuf]
      ```

entry .servables   the given which serves a `Protobuf` message as an HTTP response body
  detail
      A choice package of `locomotion.http` (JVM only), imported by name:
      `import servables.protobufServable`.
  entry .protobufServable   `Protobuf is Servable`: a fixed body of the wire bytes, typed `application/protobuf`

entry .construables   the given which reads an `application/protobuf` request body as a `Protobuf`
  detail
      A choice package of `locomotion.http` (JVM only), imported by name:
      `import construables.protobufConstruable`.
  entry .protobufConstruable   `"application/protobuf" is Construable to Protobuf`: the body's bytes as a message
      Once constructed, the message is decoded with `as[T]`, or the request body read with
      `read[T in Protobuf]` directly, so a binary API is consumed with the same code as a
      JSON one; only the format's type differs.
